## T1 — `SessionManager` core (create / close / handle / restore-stub)

**Files touched:** `crates/harness-runtime/src/session_manager.rs` (new), `crates/harness-runtime/src/lib.rs` (add one `pub mod` line)
**Depends on:** none (Phase 4 complete is the only precondition)
**Effort:** M

`SessionManager` owns the registry `SessionId -> Arc<SessionRuntime>`. It does **not** resolve backends/tools/workspaces itself — that remains `harness-engine`'s job (dependency direction: engine → runtime, never the reverse). It accepts already-resolved dependencies and is responsible only for id issuance, registration, and lookup.

```rust
// crates/harness-runtime/src/session_manager.rs
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::sync::Arc;
use tokio::sync::RwLock;

use harness_protocol::ids::SessionId;
use harness_protocol::tools::AgentToolset;

use crate::session_runtime::SessionRuntime;
use crate::traits::{EventSink, ExecutionBackend, ToolRegistry, Workspace};

#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]
pub enum SessionManagerError {
    #[error("session {0} not found")]
    NotFound(SessionId),
    #[error("session restore is not yet supported (Phase 7)")]
    RestoreNotSupported,
}

pub struct SessionManager {
    sessions: RwLock<HashMap<SessionId, Arc<SessionRuntime>>>,
}

impl SessionManager {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        Self { sessions: RwLock::new(HashMap::new()) }
    }

    pub async fn create_session(
        &self,
        backend: Arc<dyn ExecutionBackend>,
        tool_registry: Arc<dyn ToolRegistry>,
        workspace: Arc<dyn Workspace>,
        event_sink: Arc<dyn EventSink>,
        root_toolset: AgentToolset,
    ) -> Arc<SessionRuntime> {
        let session_id = SessionId::new();
        let runtime = Arc::new(SessionRuntime::new_with_toolset(
            session_id, backend, tool_registry, workspace, event_sink, root_toolset,
        ));
        self.sessions.write().await.insert(session_id, runtime.clone());
        runtime
    }

    pub async fn session_handle(&self, id: SessionId) -> Option<Arc<SessionRuntime>> {
        self.sessions.read().await.get(&id).cloned()
    }

    pub async fn close_session(&self, id: SessionId) -> Result<(), SessionManagerError> {
        let removed = self.sessions.write().await.remove(&id);
        match removed {
            Some(runtime) => {
                runtime.cancellation.cancel();
                Ok(())
            }
            None => Err(SessionManagerError::NotFound(id)),
        }
    }

    pub async fn restore_session(
        &self,
        _id: SessionId,
    ) -> Result<Arc<SessionRuntime>, SessionManagerError> {
        Err(SessionManagerError::RestoreNotSupported)
    }

    pub async fn active_session_ids(&self) -> Vec<SessionId> {
        self.sessions.read().await.keys().copied().collect()
    }
}

impl Default for SessionManager {
    fn default() -> Self { Self::new() }
}
```

`lib.rs` gets exactly one new line: `pub mod session_manager;`.

**Acceptance criteria (unchanged from doc):** two `create_session` calls return distinct `SessionId`s with independently valid handles; `close_session` on one doesn't invalidate the other's `session_handle`.

**Why `RwLock<HashMap<..>>` and not `DashMap`:** matches the doc's own stated default and the crate's existing idiom (`SessionEventBus.receivers: Mutex<Vec<..>>`); no `dashmap` dependency needs to be added.

